title: 'C-c v ? : skg-describe-at-point'
pid: 9f585e04-4c8f-456c-b8a6-a1c9c8c0e610
body: |2-
  Says in plain sentences what the headline at point is, when it is a
  folder (what that kind of folder holds, and how its members relate to
  the note above it), then what each herald it shows means, one
  sentence each, the most informative first, with this headline's own
  numbers and letters read out: for =2/5L=, "5 notes link to this one;
  2 of them are substantive (they have a body, content, or link to
  other notes too)". It works from the headline or its body, with
  heralds shown or hidden. Two lines or fewer show in the echo area,
  more in the help buffer '*skg describe*'. The server writes the
  sentences ("Describe heralds" in 'docs/api-and-formats.org'), which
  Neovim's ':SkgDescribeAtPoint' and skg-for-ai's 'describe' show too.
  It changes nothing and saves nothing.
